---

```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: mswitch1
diagnosis_text:
The fault is on mswitch1, not router0. The VLAN 10 sub-interface eth0.10 on
mswitch1 — the 802.1Q-tagged sub-interface sitting on trunk port eth0 (the
link_msw1_sw1 segment that connects mswitch1 to switch1) — has been taken down
administratively after boot. Its kernel state is <BROADCAST,MULTICAST> (no UP
flag) and its bridge-slave state is "disabled", meaning it is fully detached
from the br_vlan10 bridge domain. As a consequence, any VLAN 10 Ethernet frame
arriving from switch1 (where PC4 at 172.16.10.4 and PC5 at 172.16.10.5 reside)
is silently dropped at mswitch1 before it can traverse the bridge to
eth4.10@eth4 (the trunk port toward router0). PC4's ICMP to 172.16.10.1 times
out with 100 % loss, confirming the data-plane break. VLAN 20 is unaffected
because mswitch1.eth0.20 on the same physical port remains UP and forwarding,
which is why laptop0 (VLAN 20) experiences no outage. Router0's eth0.10
sub-interface is fully healthy — it is UP, carries 172.16.10.1/24, and shows
both TX and RX traffic — making the team's hypothesis of a router0 VLAN 10
sub-interface failure incorrect. The mswitch1 startup script confirms the
intended state is "up" (ip link set eth0.10 up is present), so the current DOWN
state is a post-boot administrative shutdown of that sub-interface.
Root cause: mswitch1.eth0.10 was administratively shut down after lab startup,
severing VLAN 10 connectivity on the mswitch1–switch1 trunk.
Corrective fix: on mswitch1, run `ip link set eth0.10 up` to restore the
sub-interface; VLAN 10 frames will immediately resume forwarding through
br_vlan10 and PC4/PC5 will recover gateway reachability.
=== END ===
```